---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

// The disclaimer, on a route of its own.
//
// This lived at /about, which was the whole of that route: the page was titled
// "Disclaimer" and the header linked to it as such. Splitting the two gives each
// page one name in one place - the header carries About, the footer carries this
// - rather than a single file answering to both.
//
// /about is not redirected here. It still resolves, to a page about the blog, so
// an old link lands somewhere sensible rather than on a 404; what it does not do
// is land on the small print. That is the honest trade for the split, and the
// reason the legal text moved rather than the introduction.
---

<BaseLayout
	title="Disclaimer"
	description="Educational use, professional advice, third-party trademarks, liability and intellectual property terms for this blog."
	narrow
	crumbs={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'Disclaimer' }]}
>
	{
		/* Centred to match the article pages. sb-blog left-aligns its reading columns,
	    but it fills the right-hand space with a sticky table of contents; with
	    nothing there, left-aligning would leave half the page empty. */
	}
	<div class="mx-auto max-w-2xl">
		<h1 class="font-display text-text text-3xl font-bold">Disclaimer</h1>

		<div class="prose-body mt-6">
			<h2>Information and Educational Use</h2>

			<p>
				The content on this blog is published for educational and informational purposes. It
				documents my ongoing learning, research, lab work, certification studies, technical
				experiments and observations relating to Microsoft security technologies, XDR, identity,
				detection engineering and associated security topics.
			</p>

			<p>
				Although I aim to keep the material accurate and current, technologies, product features,
				interfaces, documentation and recommended practices can change. I therefore cannot guarantee
				that every article, configuration, query, diagram or example will remain complete, accurate
				or suitable for every environment.
			</p>

			<p>
				Any use of the information on this blog is at your own discretion and risk. Always validate
				configurations and technical guidance against current vendor documentation and within an
				appropriate test environment before applying them to production systems.
			</p>

			<h2>Professional Advice</h2>

			<p>
				The views and material published here reflect my own learning, research and experience at
				the time of writing.
			</p>

			<p>
				This blog is written in a personal capacity. The views expressed are my own and do not
				represent those of my employer, its clients or any other organisation I am associated with.
				Nothing published here is written on their behalf, at their direction or with their
				endorsement.
			</p>

			<p>
				Nothing on this blog should be considered professional, legal, financial, compliance or
				security advice, nor does the content create a professional or advisory relationship.
			</p>

			<p>
				Examples, architectures, security configurations, KQL queries, detection logic and lab
				exercises are provided to demonstrate concepts and may require modification before they are
				suitable for a particular organisation or environment.
			</p>

			<p>
				For decisions affecting production systems, organisational security or regulatory
				obligations, appropriate qualified professionals and current official documentation should
				be consulted.
			</p>

			<h2>Microsoft and Other Third Parties</h2>

			<p>
				This is an independent personal blog and is not affiliated with, sponsored by or endorsed by
				Microsoft or any other vendor mentioned on the site.
			</p>

			<p>
				Microsoft, Microsoft Defender, Microsoft Sentinel, Microsoft Entra and other product names,
				trademarks and logos remain the property of their respective owners.
			</p>

			<p>
				References to third-party products or services are made for educational and informational
				purposes.
			</p>

			<h2>Third-Party Links</h2>

			<p>
				Articles may contain links to Microsoft documentation, technical resources, GitHub
				repositories or other third-party websites.
			</p>

			<p>
				These external resources are maintained independently, and I have no control over their
				content, availability, security or subsequent changes. A link should not be interpreted as
				an endorsement of the website, organisation or all material published there.
			</p>

			<h2>Limitation of Liability</h2>

			<p>
				To the fullest extent permitted by applicable law, I accept no responsibility for loss,
				damage, disruption, data loss, security incidents or other consequences arising from the use
				or misuse of information, code, configurations, queries or techniques published on this
				blog.
			</p>

			<p>
				Readers are responsible for assessing whether material is appropriate for their own systems
				and circumstances.
			</p>

			<h2>Changes to This Disclaimer</h2>

			<p>
				This disclaimer may be updated periodically to reflect changes to the blog, its content or
				the technologies covered.
			</p>

			<p>The version published on this page applies to the current use of the site.</p>

			<h2>Intellectual Property</h2>

			<p>
				Unless otherwise stated, the original written content, lab documentation, diagrams, graphics
				and other materials created specifically for this blog are my own work and are protected by
				applicable intellectual property law.
			</p>

			<p>
				They may not be reproduced, republished, distributed or materially adapted for another
				website, publication, training resource or commercial purpose without my prior written
				permission.
			</p>

			<p>
				Third-party trademarks, documentation, screenshots, code, quotations and other referenced
				materials remain the property of their respective owners and are subject to their own
				licences and terms.
			</p>

			<p>
				Where external material has been used or referenced, I aim to provide appropriate
				attribution.
			</p>
		</div>
	</div>
</BaseLayout>
